{"subjectId":"java","id":"threads-basics","title":"Threads & Runnable","group":"Concurrency","order":58,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A thread is an independent path of execution inside one JVM process — create it with a <code>Runnable</code>, start it with <code>start()</code>, wait for it with <code>join()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Thread</strong> — the smallest unit the OS schedules. All threads of a process share its heap (objects, static fields) but each has its <em>own stack</em> (local variables, call frames) and program counter.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layers-difference\"></i>Process vs thread</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Process</th><th>Thread</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Own address space</td><td>Shares the process heap; own stack</td></tr><tr><td>Creation cost</td><td>Heavy (fork, new address space)</td><td>Lighter (~1 MB stack reserved for a platform thread)</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>IPC: pipes, sockets, files</td><td>Shared objects — fast, but needs synchronisation</td></tr><tr><td>Crash impact</td><td>Isolated</td><td>An uncaught error kills only that thread, but corrupted shared state affects all</td></tr><tr><td>In Java</td><td><code>ProcessBuilder</code>, one JVM = one process</td><td><code>Thread</code>, executors, virtual threads</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Creating and joining threads</h3>\n<p>Give each thread its own slice of the work, <code>start()</code> them, then <code>join()</code> before reading the results — <code>join()</code> both waits <em>and</em> guarantees you see everything the thread wrote.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        long[] sums = new long[2];\n        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -&gt; sums[0] = sum(1, 50), \"worker-1\");\n        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -&gt; sums[1] = sum(51, 100), \"worker-2\");\n        t1.start();                 // both now run concurrently with main\n        t2.start();\n        t1.join();                  // wait for each to finish\n        t2.join();\n        System.out.println(t1.getName() + \" -&gt; \" + sums[0]);\n        System.out.println(t2.getName() + \" -&gt; \" + sums[1]);\n        System.out.println(\"total = \" + (sums[0] + sums[1]));\n    }\n\n    static long sum(int from, int to) {\n        long s = 0;\n        for (int i = from; i &lt;= to; i++) s += i;\n        return s;\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>worker-1 -&gt; 1275\nworker-2 -&gt; 3775\ntotal = 5050</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Three ways to define the task</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Thread a = new Greeter();                       // 1. subclass Thread\n        a.setName(\"greeter\");\n        a.start();\n        a.join();\n\n        Runnable task = new Runnable() {                // 2. implement Runnable\n            @Override public void run() {\n                System.out.println(\"2. Runnable on \" + Thread.currentThread().getName());\n            }\n        };\n        Thread b = new Thread(task, \"runner\");\n        b.start();\n        b.join();\n\n        Thread c = new Thread(                          // 3. lambda (Runnable is functional)\n            () -&gt; System.out.println(\"3. lambda on \" + Thread.currentThread().getName()), \"lambda\");\n        c.start();\n        c.join();\n    }\n}\n\nclass Greeter extends Thread {\n    @Override public void run() {\n        System.out.println(\"1. Thread subclass on \" + getName());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1. Thread subclass on greeter\n2. Runnable on runner\n3. lambda on lambda</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Implement <code>Runnable</code> (or <code>Callable</code>) and hand it to a thread or executor — the task stays independent of <em>how</em> it is run, and your class can still extend something else.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Extending <code>Thread</code> just to override <code>run()</code> — it burns your single superclass slot and couples the work to one thread.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-play\"></i><code>start()</code> vs <code>run()</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Runnable r = () -&gt; System.out.println(\"running in \" + Thread.currentThread().getName());\n        Thread t = new Thread(r, \"worker\");\n        t.run();     // an ordinary method call — runs on main, no new thread\n        t.start();   // creates the new call stack and calls run() there\n        t.join();\n        try {\n            t.start();   // a Thread object can be started only once\n        } catch (IllegalThreadStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"second start(): \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>running in main\nrunning in worker\nsecond start(): IllegalThreadStateException</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-refresh\"></i>Thread lifecycle</h3>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>NEW</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>RUNNABLE</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>BLOCKED</code> / <code>WAITING</code> / <code>TIMED_WAITING</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>RUNNABLE</code></div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\"><code>TERMINATED</code></div></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>Thread.State</code></th><th>Meaning</th><th>Entered by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NEW</code></td><td>Created, not yet started</td><td><code>new Thread(...)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>RUNNABLE</code></td><td>Running <em>or</em> ready to run (the JVM doesn't distinguish)</td><td><code>start()</code>; also while doing blocking I/O</td></tr><tr><td><code>BLOCKED</code></td><td>Waiting to acquire a monitor lock</td><td>entering a <code>synchronized</code> block held by another thread</td></tr><tr><td><code>WAITING</code></td><td>Waiting indefinitely for another thread</td><td><code>wait()</code>, <code>join()</code>, <code>LockSupport.park()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>TIMED_WAITING</code></td><td>Waiting with a timeout</td><td><code>sleep(ms)</code>, <code>wait(ms)</code>, <code>join(ms)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>TERMINATED</code></td><td><code>run()</code> returned or threw</td><td>end of <code>run()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Object lock = new Object();\n        Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            synchronized (lock) { System.out.println(\"worker got the lock\"); }\n        });\n        System.out.println(\"before start: \" + t.getState());\n        synchronized (lock) {                      // main holds the lock...\n            t.start();\n            while (t.getState() != Thread.State.BLOCKED) Thread.onSpinWait();\n            System.out.println(\"waiting for lock: \" + t.getState());\n        }                                          // ...and releases it here\n        t.join();\n        System.out.println(\"after join: \" + t.getState());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>before start: NEW\nwaiting for lock: BLOCKED\nworker got the lock\nafter join: TERMINATED</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hand-stop\"></i><code>sleep</code>, interrupt and daemon threads</h3>\n<p>Java has no safe way to <em>kill</em> a thread (<code>stop()</code> is deprecated and removed in practice). Instead you <strong>interrupt</strong> it — a polite request the thread must check for. Blocking calls (<code>sleep</code>, <code>wait</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>BlockingQueue.take</code>) respond by throwing <code>InterruptedException</code> and <em>clearing</em> the flag.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        Thread sleeper = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            try {\n                Thread.sleep(10_000);\n                System.out.println(\"woke normally\");\n            } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n                System.out.println(\"sleep interrupted, flag now \" + Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted());\n            }\n        });\n        sleeper.start();\n        sleeper.interrupt();\n        sleeper.join();\n\n        Thread spinner = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            long n = 0;\n            while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) n++;   // cooperative check\n            System.out.println(\"spinner saw the interrupt and stopped\");\n        });\n        spinner.start();\n        spinner.interrupt();\n        spinner.join();\n\n        Thread daemon = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            while (true) {\n                try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { return; }\n            }\n        });\n        daemon.setDaemon(true);          // must be set before start()\n        daemon.start();\n        System.out.println(\"daemon? \" + daemon.isDaemon() + \" - JVM exits without waiting for it\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>sleep interrupted, flag now false\nspinner saw the interrupt and stopped\ndaemon? true - JVM exits without waiting for it</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Thread.sleep(ms)</code></td><td>Pause the <em>current</em> thread; keeps any locks it holds</td></tr><tr><td><code>t.join()</code> / <code>t.join(ms)</code></td><td>Wait until <code>t</code> terminates (happens-before: you see all its writes)</td></tr><tr><td><code>t.interrupt()</code></td><td>Set <code>t</code>'s interrupt flag; wakes it from <code>sleep</code>/<code>wait</code>/<code>join</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Thread.interrupted()</code></td><td><em>Static</em>: test <strong>and clear</strong> the current thread's flag</td></tr><tr><td><code>t.isInterrupted()</code></td><td>Test the flag without clearing it</td></tr><tr><td><code>t.setDaemon(true)</code></td><td>Background thread — the JVM exits when only daemons remain</td></tr><tr><td><code>Thread.yield()</code></td><td>Hint to the scheduler; practically never needed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Never swallow <code>InterruptedException</code> with an empty <code>catch</code>. Either propagate it, or restore the flag with <code>Thread.currentThread().interrupt()</code> so code higher up can see the request to stop.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between start() and run()?</summary><div><p><code>start()</code> asks the JVM to create a new thread, which then calls <code>run()</code> on its own stack. Calling <code>run()</code> directly is just a normal method call on the <em>current</em> thread — no concurrency. <code>start()</code> may be called only once per <code>Thread</code> object (<code>IllegalThreadStateException</code> otherwise).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Runnable vs Callable?</summary><div><p><code>Runnable.run()</code> returns <code>void</code> and cannot throw checked exceptions. <code>Callable&lt;V&gt;.call()</code> returns a value and may throw <code>Exception</code>. Threads accept only <code>Runnable</code>; executors accept both and return a <code>Future</code> for either.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you stop a thread?</summary><div><p>Cooperatively: call <code>interrupt()</code> (or set a <code>volatile</code> flag) and have the thread check <code>isInterrupted()</code> / catch <code>InterruptedException</code> and return. <code>Thread.stop()</code> is deprecated because it releases locks mid-update and leaves objects corrupted.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does sleep() release the lock?</summary><div><p>No. <code>sleep()</code> keeps every monitor the thread holds. <code>wait()</code> is the one that releases the monitor of the object it is called on while waiting.</p></div></details></section>"}